(B) §4 STUDY-LEVEL CLASSES — SEVEN DECLARED
=====================================================================

CENSUS_COMPLETE [*] | Concrete corpus input K1: the two seats' enumerations agree on 40 included claims and a recorded exclusion ledger; every included claim states an equation or procedure; every input of every claim is PRINTED, STANDARD, or PRINTED-via-IMPORTED with a machine match at the named source's cited line; every attempt completes under 120 s; each claim therefore files exactly one of REPRO_EXACT / REPRO_FAILED (see the dichotomy in the suspicion answer below). All controls C0–C5b pass in both seats; no control splits; enumerations and input lists agree (merge exit 0); origin disagreements affect no more than 10% of included claims; the external custodian's seed is supplied and recorded with the receipt; the C6 audit reproduces every arithmetic-group claim, the sampled remainder, and the candidate/exclusion ledgers; Blanc's post-opening re-hash verifies all four values against receipts P and T. | §4.1 definition: "every included claim carries exactly one outcome from the arithmetic group of §3" — satisfied by construction in K1. §4 precedence: each earlier class's condition fails to hold (no control failure → not R3C2_NO_CLASS; no split → not CENSUS_CONTROL_SPLIT; enumerations and input lists agree → not CENSUS_DENOMINATOR_DISPUTED; origin splits ≤10% → not CENSUS_ORIGIN_DISPUTED; audit and receipts verify → not CENSUS_AUDIT_FAILED; zero non-arithmetic outcomes → not CENSUS_PARTIAL) → `CENSUS_COMPLETE` files. "Report the full tally with its denominator, and the `rests_on` tally beside it — two tallies from one pass." | reachable: YES

CENSUS_PARTIAL | Concrete corpus input K2: exactly as K1 except one included claim — paper_F.txt line 130, the ABSENT Ω_r of A1 — files REPRO_INPUT_ABSENT. The one permitted repeat (§4.2) is "meaningful only for `REPRO_NOT_EVALUABLE`," so the absent-input claim's outcome survives the repeat. | §4.2: "after the §2 attempt (one repeat permitted...), at least one included claim carries a non-arithmetic outcome (`REPRO_NO_DERIVATION_STATED`, `REPRO_INPUT_ABSENT`, `REPRO_BLOCKED`, `REPRO_NOT_EVALUABLE`). Report each and why." → `CENSUS_PARTIAL`. "INCONCLUSIVE, and it takes precedence over `CENSUS_COMPLETE`" — and the §4 filing order places CENSUS_PARTIAL immediately before CENSUS_COMPLETE, so the two can never co-file. | reachable: YES

CENSUS_AUDIT_FAILED | Concrete input AF1 (audit limb): after the tally is sealed, the custodian supplies the seed; the third seat's C6 audit re-derives claim X — drawn by `random.Random(seed_int).sample(remaining_ids, k)` per C6, or any arithmetic-group claim, which is audited without sampling discount — and cannot reproduce its filed outcome; alternatively the audit finds the candidate or exclusion ledger incomplete against the pinned sources. Concrete input AF2 (seal limb): any of the four values fails Blanc's post-opening re-hash against receipts P and T, or a receipt is missing (§7: "Any missing receipt or mismatch files `CENSUS_AUDIT_FAILED`... and voids the comparison"); or the seed is never supplied — C6: "the audit does not run, `C6_AUDIT_SAMPLE=NOT_RUN`, and the study files `CENSUS_AUDIT_FAILED` with the missing seed named." | §4.3: "the audit of §6 cannot reproduce a sampled per-claim outcome or ledger, or the receipt verification of the seal fails. No tally is filed; report which." C6: "Any outcome the audit cannot reproduce, or any ledger incompleteness, files `CENSUS_AUDIT_FAILED`." §4 precedence places it after the dispute classes and before PARTIAL/COMPLETE; §4.4's carve-out keeps it out of R3C2_NO_CLASS ("A C6 audit failure or a seal-receipt failure files `CENSUS_AUDIT_FAILED`, not this class"). | reachable: YES

R3C2_NO_CLASS | Concrete input NC1 (in-run limb): control C2 fails in every seat that attempted it, after two attempts — each seat's ledger fails `/usr/bin/python3 r3c2_ledger_tools.py validate <ledger.json> .` (non-zero exit, e.g. a PRINTED value that does not machine-match its cited line) on both tries. Concrete input NC2 (pre-dispatch limb): the packet builder's assertion finds a forbidden-list string surviving → `C4_PACKET_REDACTED=FAIL`, "the packet is not written and... the study does not proceed on a hand-checked copy." | §4.4: "a control among C0 through C5b fails in every seat that attempted it after two attempts; a packet or seat-isolation failure before dispatch files this class." → `R3C2_NO_CLASS`. §4 precedence: first — every later limb is unreached and its controls NOT_RUN. Carve-out honoured: C6 audit failures and seal-receipt failures route to CENSUS_AUDIT_FAILED, not here. | reachable: YES

CENSUS_DENOMINATOR_DISPUTED | Concrete input DD1 (enumeration limb): seat A includes candidate passage p (paper_G.txt line 64 prints a numeral A reads as the paper's own result under §1); seat B excludes it as ATTRIBUTED_NOT_DERIVED; the disagreement survives two reconciliation attempts. Concrete input DD2 (input-list limb, V16's addition): the seats agree on every candidate, but their input lists for an agreed claim differ; `merge` exits 1 ("exit 1 if their `input_id` sets differ"); the one reconciliation against the paper's stated equation is held; the difference survives — "an input-set difference surviving that reconciliation stops the study under `CENSUS_DENOMINATOR_DISPUTED` (§4), the disputed inputs listed with both seats' quotations" (C3; kimi F1, §10.10). | §1: "disagreement on any candidate that survives two reconciliation attempts stops the study under `CENSUS_DENOMINATOR_DISPUTED` (§4)." §4.5: "the two enumerations disagree after two reconciliation attempts, or the two seats' input lists for the agreed claims disagree after the one C3 reconciliation. The census does not proceed; the disputed candidates or inputs are listed." §6 limb A restates the stop by name. | reachable: YES

CENSUS_ORIGIN_DISPUTED | Concrete input OD1: 40 included claims; on 5 of them — more than 10% — the two seats' independent origin classifications split, e.g. the sentence "We adopt H₀ = 67.4 from Planck (2018)" filed IMPORTED (ORIG_CITATION) by seat A under the reason-code precedence ("a sentence that names an external source for the value is a citation whatever else it says," C3) and CHOSEN (ORIG_CHOICE_STATED) by seat B. The merge carries `origin_alt`/`origin_evidence_alt`; affected claims carry `rests_on` as a pair marked DISPUTED. Boundary exhibited: at exactly 4 of 40 claims (10%, not "more than 10%") the class does not file and the census proceeds with DISPUTED marks. | §4.6: "the two seats' independent `origin` classifications disagree on inputs affecting more than 10% of included claims. The census does not proceed; every disputed input is listed with both seats' classification and both quotations." C6: "Above 10% of included claims, `CENSUS_ORIGIN_DISPUTED`"; the disagreement is "reported, never reconciled." | reachable: YES

CENSUS_CONTROL_SPLIT | Concrete input CS1: control C5 passes in seat A's run (all three commands exit 0, full stdout printed) and fails in seat B's run (the sympy import exits non-zero), each after two attempts. The both-seats-fail class cannot see this state — that is the gap this class was added to close (§4.7's note: the old wording "said 'in both seats', leaving a control that failed twice in one seat and passed in the other with no class"). | §4.7: "a control fails in one seat and passes in another after two attempts. Report both seats' outputs and stop; do not adopt the passing seat's result." → `CENSUS_CONTROL_SPLIT`. §4 precedence places it second, after R3C2_NO_CLASS. | reachable: YES

