(C) EXPLICIT REACHABILITY, ONE LINE EACH
=====================================================================
§3: REPRO_EXACT — reachable. REPRO_FAILED — reachable. REPRO_BLOCKED — reachable. REPRO_NOT_EVALUABLE — reachable. REPRO_NO_DERIVATION_STATED — reachable. REPRO_INPUT_ABSENT — reachable.
§4: CENSUS_COMPLETE — reachable (conditional on corpus content; see the suspicion answer). CENSUS_PARTIAL — reachable. CENSUS_AUDIT_FAILED — reachable. R3C2_NO_CLASS — reachable. CENSUS_DENOMINATOR_DISPUTED — reachable. CENSUS_ORIGIN_DISPUTED — reachable. CENSUS_CONTROL_SPLIT — reachable.
No declared outcome or class is UNREACHABLE.

=====================================================================
THE SUSPICION, ANSWERED DIRECTLY
Is there any input under which CENSUS_COMPLETE can actually be filed — or does a single blocked, absent-input, or no-derivation-stated claim anywhere in the corpus force CENSUS_PARTIAL, making CENSUS_COMPLETE unreachable in practice?
=====================================================================
Answer: REACHABLE.

The routing, from the text:
1. §4.1 defines the class: "every included claim carries exactly one outcome from the arithmetic group of §3."
2. §3 defines the arithmetic group: "exactly `REPRO_EXACT` and `REPRO_FAILED`."
3. §2 step 4 licenses the only path into that group — "follow the paper's own recipe, using every value it directs you to use, i.e. every ledger record with status `PRINTED` or `STANDARD`" — and the attempt terminates by dichotomy: the reproduced value either rounds to the printed numeral at the stated precision (REPRO_EXACT) or it does not (REPRO_FAILED). §3 declares no third arithmetic outcome, so every claim whose inputs are all PRINTED/STANDARD and whose attempt completes lands in the group.
4. The clause that decides the suspicion, quoted verbatim, is §4.2: "at least one included claim carries a non-arithmetic outcome (`REPRO_NO_DERIVATION_STATED`, `REPRO_INPUT_ABSENT`, `REPRO_BLOCKED`, `REPRO_NOT_EVALUABLE`). Report each and why. INCONCLUSIVE, and it takes precedence over `CENSUS_COMPLETE`." — reinforced by the §4 filing order: "...`CENSUS_AUDIT_FAILED`, `CENSUS_PARTIAL`, `CENSUS_COMPLETE`."
5. So the premise is correct: a single blocked, absent-input, no-derivation-stated, or not-evaluable claim anywhere in the corpus forces CENSUS_PARTIAL, and the precedence makes that filing exclusive. The document itself flags exactly this sensitivity (§5 C0 note: "CENSUS_COMPLETE requires every included claim to carry an arithmetic-group outcome, which a single blocked or absent input in the whole corpus is enough to prevent").
6. But nothing in the text forces any such claim to exist. The four capturing classes are input-contingent, not design-mandatory: the K1 corpus exhibited above — every claim with a stated recipe, every input PRINTED, STANDARD, or PRINTED-via-IMPORTED with a machine match, every attempt completing — produces zero non-arithmetic outcomes, and §4.1 then files. That input is constructible under the document's own rules; the design licenses it.
7. Contrast with the one genuinely unreachable class this study has produced: V9's REPRO_AFTER_CHOICE under option (b), where the method licensed no procedure that could ever file it — §10.3's blocking clause: "the mandated attempt may not consume that input, and no second attempt is specified." No clause of V16 does that to CENSUS_COMPLETE. Its reachability is empirical (does the pinned corpus in fact contain such a claim — the question the census exists to answer), not logical (can any admissible corpus avoid them — C0's question). The latter is yes.
Verdict: reachable.

